<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.local.claude-codex-memory-bridge-doctor</string>

	<!-- TEMPLATE. Not installed by install_bridge.py, and installing it is a change to
	     the user's own machine configuration, so it is theirs to make. See README.md,
	     "Periodic health check", for what it does and does not buy you.

	     Runs `install_bridge.py doctor`: read-only, never writes, never touches a
	     hooks.json. It exists for ONE failure class, the one that actually happened —
	     on 2026-08-21 a Codex app upgrade deleted ~/.codex/hooks.json and this machine
	     ran the original, unbounded, pre-round-1 vulnerable redact() in production for
	     about a week with nobody noticing.

	     INTERPRETER: /opt/homebrew/bin/python3, and this is load-bearing, not a
	     preference. Every path this tool manages is on /Volumes/Extreme SSD by design
	     (resolve_ssd_path enforces it), and macOS TCC denies Apple platform binaries
	     access to removable volumes inside a launchd session. Measured on this machine
	     under a real throwaway launchd job (ego-reaper-launchd/README.md, 2026-08-28):

	         /bin/cat                    reading a source on /Volumes/...   DENIED (Errno 1)
	         /usr/bin/python3            running a source on /Volumes/...   DENIED (exit 2)
	         /opt/homebrew/bin/python3   running a source on /Volumes/...   OK

	     `/usr/bin/python3` is additionally a stub that re-execs into Xcode, so it is
	     denied AND fragile. Swapping this line to it turns this job into a health check
	     that is itself silently dead — the exact bug it is here to catch. `doctor`
	     reports `launchd_tcc_risk: true` when it notices it is running that way.

	     StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath are on INTERNAL storage for the same reason:
	     a log the job cannot write is a job you cannot diagnose. Create the directory
	     first — launchd does not, and a missing parent makes the job fail to spawn:
	         mkdir -p ~/.local/state/claude-codex-memory-bridge

	     doctor exits non-zero on any finding, so LastExitStatus in `launchctl list
	     com.local.claude-codex-memory-bridge-doctor` is a usable signal on its own. It
	     does NOT notify anybody: nothing here reads that status for you. Wire it to
	     whatever this machine already uses for alerts, or check it by hand — an
	     unwatched red light is what the original incident was.

	     Install:
	         cp com.local.claude-codex-memory-bridge-doctor.plist.template \
	            ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.local.claude-codex-memory-bridge-doctor.plist
	         # then replace __REPO__ below with this checkout's absolute path
	         launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
	            ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.local.claude-codex-memory-bridge-doctor.plist
	     Remove:
	         launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.local.claude-codex-memory-bridge-doctor
	-->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/opt/homebrew/bin/python3</string>
		<string>__REPO__/install_bridge.py</string>
		<string>doctor</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Hourly. The incident it catches is an app upgrade deleting a hooks.json, which
	     is rare and not urgent to the minute; the reaper next door runs every 60s
	     because it is reclaiming live resources, which this is not. -->
	<key>StartInterval</key>
	<integer>3600</integer>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Background</string>
	<key>LowPriorityIO</key>
	<true/>
	<key>Umask</key>
	<integer>63</integer>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>__HOME__/.local/state/claude-codex-memory-bridge/doctor.stdout.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>__HOME__/.local/state/claude-codex-memory-bridge/doctor.stderr.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
